{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "visit_duration_min",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "clinical_complexity",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Complexity of the clinical situation is a plausible candidate cause of longer visit duration; it directly represents a 'clinical factor' being attributed."
    },
    {
      "name": "acuity_level",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Acuity duplicates the existing 'urgency' column already in the table and is implied by the query's clinical-factor framing. It plausibly drives both intervention/diagnostic workload and duration, but it is essentially named in-context, so it does not demonstrate surfacing capability."
    },
    {
      "name": "diagnostic_workload",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Ordering imaging/labs or performing procedures lies on the causal path between a clinical condition and longer visits -- it is a mechanism producing extra time, not a common cause. Adjusting for it would block the effect being studied."
    },
    {
      "name": "intervention_type",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The type of intervention performed is a downstream response to the clinical situation that consumes visit time; it sits on the causal path to duration rather than being a pre-treatment common cause."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["clinical_complexity"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "acuity_level", "named_in_query": true, "plausibility": 0.5}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.2,
  "rationale": "The set does supply a plausible treatment (clinical_complexity) and outcome (visit_duration_min), but the causal-estimation setup is weak. Two of the four added columns (diagnostic_workload, intervention_type) are mediators on the path from clinical condition to longer visits, not confounders; adjusting for them would bias the attribution downward. The only variable functioning as a common cause is acuity_level, and that is essentially a restatement of the pre-existing 'urgency' column and is implied by the query, so it is named_in_query=true and does not demonstrate the capability under test. No genuine, newly surfaced confounder (e.g., patient comorbidity burden, physician/provider, time-of-day, department staffing) was introduced. Hence confounder quality is low."
}
```

